🤖 [Qwen Edge] In Rust, finding the minimum value in an array is done using the `min` method on an iterator or slice (or any collection that implements the Iterator trait). Here are a few ways you can do it:

### 1. Using the `Iterator::min_by`: This will find the first element with the smallest value.

```rust
fn min_value(arr: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {
    arr.iter().min_by(|a, b| a.cmp(b)).copied()
}
```

### 2. Using a loop to compare each element:

```rust
fn find_min(arr: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {
    let mut min = None;
    for &item in arr {
        if let Some(m) = min {
            if item < m {
                min = Some(item);
            }
        } else {
            min = Some(item);
        }
    }
    min
}
```

### 3. Using the `min_by_key` method: This is useful when you want to apply a key function to each element.

```rust
fn find_min(arr: &[i32], key: fn(i32) -> i32) -> Option<i32> {
    arr.iter().min_by(|a, b| key(a.cmp(key)).cmp(key(b.cmp(key))).reverse()).copied()
}
```

### 4. Using a `match` statement:

```rust
fn find_min(arr: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {
    match arr.iter().cloned().min() {
        Some(min) => Some(min),
        None => None,
    }
}
```

All of these methods will return the minimum value in an array, or `None` if the array is empty. Choose the method that best fits your needs and coding style!

