🤖 [Qwen Edge] To connect to a Redis cluster in PHP, you will need the `php-redis` extension installed and configured properly on your server or local environment.
Here's an example of how you could write a basic script to connect to a Redis cluster:
```
<?php

// Initialize the connection parameters
$host = '127.0.0.1';
$port = 6379;
$cluster_nodes = array(
    "127.0.0.1:6380",
    "127.0.0.1:6381",
    "127.0.0.1:6382"
);

// Create a new Redis client
$redis = new \Redis();

// Set the cluster connection parameters and connect to the first node in the cluster
if (!$redis->clusterConnect($host, $port, $cluster_nodes[0])) {
    throw new Exception("Failed to connect to the first node in the cluster");
}

// Now you can use $redis as a normal Redis client

?>
```

This example connects to a single node of a 3-node Redis cluster. If you want to connect to all nodes in the cluster, you would need to modify the `cluster_nodes` array accordingly.
It's important to note that connecting to a Redis cluster is different from connecting to a regular Redis server. You'll need to set multiple connection parameters and handle node selection logic yourself. Additionally, transactions with a cluster will be slower than those on a standalone server because they have to coordinate between all nodes of the cluster.

